# Optional empirical demonstration

Work on one candidate: open the test with `research` `begin` `{phase: "test", subjects:
[candidate]}` and settle it at the end with `research` `test`. Read it with `research_query`
`node`, including any test the analysis wrote into the evidence as the one that would settle a
question. An analytically validated candidate is the most severe untested scenario of its root
flaw's group: demonstrate it end to end at its assessed severity, since a confirmation also
covers the group's scenarios that are no more severe. An unproven one is tested for its
premises, as the last section says.

Define a minimal demonstration with explicit preconditions and observable success criteria that
distinguish the violation under test from an unrelated error, and a control run on the same
build showing the guard holds without the trigger (the benign input handled, the unauthorized
request refused), recorded as a `control` artifact beside the `attack` artifact. For a
multi-flaw chain, exercise how the flaws combine and the final consequence, not just isolated
flaws; for a chain across actions, perform each action as its own actor, in order, from the
state the last left. Use synthetic inputs and non-sensitive markers; do not expose real secrets
to demonstrate access.

Repeat the demonstration from a clean state and record the success rate. For a race, a check
apart from its use or a measured difference such as timing, fix the number of trials first and
run the control as often (the same harness without the competing flow, or with it serialised;
the same measurement on a value that should not differ), stating both counts in the note, with
the driver and any delay shim as `harness` and per-trial logs beside `attack` and `control`. It
is confirmed when the attack succeeds and its control never does, or a measured difference
separates from its control beyond a threshold stated before the runs, and an `observation` shows
the mechanism: a race detector's report, a replayable recording of the failing schedule or the
raw measurements, described with its location when too large to copy. Widening the window (a
delay at the check, CPU pinning, a stalled file system) counts only for a window present in the
unmodified build, disclosed, with the success rate of a run without it. Show that the fault
originates from the candidate's trigger, not from the harness. Flags or settings that disable
the target's own protections invalidate the demonstration unless the class is about those
protections. Use an ephemeral port per harness, never a fixed default.

Check revision, configuration, endpoint ownership, shared state and privileges before
execution. Selection for a test is not blanket approval; do not broaden the target to obtain a
result. Make an environment change the candidate's premises admit (a service, a build or a
non-default option) yourself: one only this test needs in your own instance, recorded as
provenance; a reusable one through an `environment` child briefed with this candidate.

Delegate work that stands apart: independent approaches in parallel, building or instrumenting a
harness or build, long trial runs, environment changes, and side questions that need deep code
reading. Brief each child with the candidate and its exact sub-task;
children keep their files in their working directories and their evidence in the store: work
from those records, not their replies.

Save the smallest reproducible payload, sanitized commands and configuration, expected versus
actual observations, and relevant logs or traces in your working directory.
Re-run after every reduction; keep the smallest payload that still demonstrates the impact and
write human-followable ordered steps into the test's note. Before recording, enumerate the
artifacts the demonstration needs — one `attack`, one `control`, an `observation` per step that
produces evidence — and check that each exists:

```js
{% include "security/scripts/poc-artifacts.js" %}
```

Keep going until the demonstration confirms the scenario or disproves it. An inconclusive run is
not a result: refine the approach, harness, instrumentation, environment or trial counts, and
run again. Record the result with `research` `test`, with its artifacts and the environment it
ran in. Confirm only when the demonstration meets its success criteria. Record `failed` only
with evidence that the scenario does not work as formulated, recorded as contradicting evidence
on the chain flaw or premise it bears on, which sends the candidate back to review. A demonstration
waiting on the user (an approval, credentials, a destination or privilege, an option outside the
premises) is `pending`, stating exactly what it waits for. Failure to demonstrate is not proof
that no vulnerability exists.

Settle this test on this candidate's scenario alone: another input along the same path to the
same consequence is this scenario, to try in this test; anything else the run shows is recorded
apart, citing its artifacts, when you find it. Return the authoritative result and
evidence references, and the IDs you recorded.

## An unproven candidate

Its review left code premises of its chain undecided that reading could not settle: observe
them rather than demonstrate the scenario. Run the demonstration plan its review recorded as
context evidence on it, or a bounded plan of your own, against those premises, with the care
above. Propose each premise you observe with `research` `set_status`: `holds`, or `fails` on an
observed counterexample, citing the run's artifacts, for its settle to decide; record the test
`confirmed`, or `failed` when one fails. A plan that shows nothing ends the test: record what
was tried as context evidence on each premise and the test as `unavailable`. That is no proof either way, so it
contradicts nothing.
